<?nvs
// The end of a script, observed from inside it.
//
// [ADR 0127](/docs/decisions/0127.md) is the
// design. `Core\Script::onExit` registers a hook that runs as the **last user
// code of the script**, at every non-fatal ending: the last statement, an
// `exit`, or a throw that reached the root. The hook is handed a readonly
// report saying which of those it was and what status the process will carry,
// and it cannot change either — a hook observes an ending rather than steering
// one.
//
// This program takes the first of the three. Both hooks are registered before
// any work happens, and neither runs until the last line below has already
// run: `work done` is printed by the script, and the two lines after it are
// printed by the queue, in the order the hooks were registered.
//
// `exit` is the ending this exists for. A root `try`/`finally` covers a normal
// end and an uncaught throw, and PHP skips `finally` on `exit` exactly as
// Novis does — so before this queue there was no way at all to run code at the
// one ending a CLI program most wants to clean up after.
//
// The three lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    // The reason is an ordinary enum, so a `match` over it is exhaustive and a
    // fourth ending would be a compile error here rather than a silent branch.
    // There is deliberately no case for a `FATAL` or a cancellation: neither
    // runs a hook, so neither has a reason to report.
    string $reason = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => 'Normal',
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => 'ExitCall',
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => 'UncaughtThrow',
    };
    echo 'onExit: ', $reason, ' ', $report->status(), "\n";
});

// Registering twice registers twice — this is a queue and not a slot, which is
// what makes it usable by two unrelated parts of one program.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (): void => {
    // A hook may declare no parameter at all, exactly as a `Core\Fatal`
    // handler may: what it is handed is decided at the call.
    echo "second hook\n";
});

echo "work done\n";
